    {
      "id": "u5hc82c",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Volcanoes"
    },
    {
      "id": "9bw00wc",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "6cafnak",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GEN AWARENESS/images/image_6cafnak.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Volcanoes are categorized by eruptive activity into Active, Dormant, and Extinct types.",
        "Volcanic shapes include Shield Volcanoes, Composite Volcanoes (Stratovolcanoes), and Cinder Cones.",
        "Seismic waves caused by earthquakes are divided into Body Waves (Primary/P-waves and Secondary/S-waves) and Surface Waves (L-waves).",
        "Earthquake magnitude measures energy released on the logarithmic Richter Scale, whereas intensity measures physical destruction on the Mercalli Scale.",
        "The Circum-Pacific Belt, known as the 'Ring of Fire', hosts the world's highest concentration of active volcanoes and seismic zones."
      ],
      "id": "qe6ez5l"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2jx5h0d",
      "content": "A volcano is an opening in the Earth's crust through which molten rock (magma), gases, and ash are expelled to the surface. When magma reaches the Earth's surface, it is called lava."
    },
    {
      "id": "gqzqjv0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Structure of a Volcano"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ax9rvyo",
      "content": "• Crater – bowl-shaped opening at the top of a volcano."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tst24v1",
      "content": "• Vent – the channel through which magma reaches the surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g0em4wh",
      "content": "• Magma Chamber – underground reservoir of molten rock."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ft6hcmv",
      "content": "• Lava Flow – molten rock flowing out during eruption."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uykwdxr",
      "content": "• Ash and Gas – volcanic eruptions release ash, steam, and gases."
    },
    {
