    {
      "id": "xoui5ma",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Causes of Earthquakes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kmqhld6",
      "content": "• Movement of tectonic plates."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6hqe4ai",
      "content": "• Volcanic activity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tzlukoy",
      "content": "• Faulting in Earth's crust."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "elt4ig4",
      "content": "• Human activities such as mining and dam construction."
    },
    {
      "id": "buizg3y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Measurement of Earthquakes"
    },
    {
      "id": "wdek9s9",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Scale</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Richter Scale</p>",
            "<p>Measures magnitude (energy released).</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mercalli Scale</p>",
            "<p>Measures intensity based on damage observed.</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mm7jyus",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Effects of Earthquakes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5b1h4lg",
      "content": "• Damage to buildings and infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hr90emw",
      "content": "• Landslides and ground cracks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6gdk5wd",
      "content": "• Tsunamis in coastal regions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5284nhj",
      "content": "• Loss of life and property."
    },
    {
      "id": "w4pwe51",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Major Earthquake Zones of the World"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "P-waves can travel through solid, liquid, and gaseous mediums, whereas S-waves can travel through solids only.",
        "The Richter Scale measures earthquake magnitude (energy released), whereas the Mercalli Scale measures damage intensity.",
        "The point inside the Earth where an earthquake originates is called the Focus (Hypocenter); the point directly above it on the Earth's surface is the Epicenter."
      ],
      "id": "1aaq9eg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "npodj41",
      "content": "• Pacific Ring of Fire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tcs7upn",
      "content": "• Mid-Atlantic Ridge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ht7nvu2",
      "content": "• Himalayan belt."
    },
    {
      "id": "y0iue8s",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "px58ncc",
      "content": "• Pacific Ring of Fire has the highest number of volcanoes and earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "047pf0m",
      "content": "• P waves travel through solids and liquids; S waves travel only through solids."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eqa73c7",
      "content": "• Epicenter is directly above the earthquake focus."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hm0szgr",
      "content": "• Richter scale measures magnitude of earthquakes."
    }
  ],
